Dear Meraj

thanks. These questions are important, but for the record let's note 
that they are not really specific for cellHTS2 or RNAi, they are 
elementary statistics questions that apply to any assay, and which are 
explained quite well in the literature.

To estimate FPR, you need a control set of 'ground truth' (i.e. 
independently determined) negatives.
To estimate TPR, you need a control set of positives.
To estimate FDR, you need both.
Ideally, you have an independent follow-up assay that allows you to 
validate both a sample of negatives and of positive hits from the screen.

If you have these control sets, then the usual estimators (such as those 
that you found on Wikipedia) apply. In addition to consulting online 
resources, old-fashioned statistics textbooks can still be useful.

As always, the value of a point estimator is not usually the same as the 
true value, for estimating its uncertainty you can resort e.g. to 
resampling.

	Best wishes
	Wolfgang



